  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Doing what it takes to stop tuberculosis in Suriname - DOTS Suriname Project - Suriname (01-Nov-2012-31-Dec-2015)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">At 533 per 100,000 inhabitants in 2007, the estimated incidence of new smear-positive tuberculosis (TB) cases in Suriname is the fifth highest in the Americas and estimated HIV prevalence among TB cases is the third highest in the region (WHO report Global Tuberculosis Control 2009). The goal of the program supported by this grant is to introduce quality-assured DOTS (the basic package that underpins the Stop TB Strategy) in Suriname in order to reduce TB morbidity, mortality and disease transmission while preventing the development of drug resistance. The program &#x2013; which is aligned with the interim national TB strategy and the global Stop TB Strategy &#x2013; will focus on fighting TB/HIV co-infections, improving infection control in health care settings and providing TB control activities for high-risk groups such as people living with HIV, prisoners and indigenous peoples. This is Suriname&#x2019;s first Global Fund grant for TB.</narrative>
  </description>
